theory.intro:
A **segment** is the region between a chord and the arc it cuts off. A minor segment's area is the sector area minus the area of the triangle formed by the two radii. For the minor-segment formula and the corresponding major-segment complement, use the minor central angle $0<\theta\leq\pi$. The segment-perimeter formula below is for the minor segment. Work in radians throughout.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (60:3);
\coordinate (B) at (-60:3);
\fill[gray!35] (B) arc (-60:60:3) -- cycle;
\draw (O) circle (3);
\draw[thick] (O) -- (A) node[midway, above left] {$r$};
\draw[thick] (O) -- (B) node[midway, below left] {$r$};
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B);
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.8cm, angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle=B--O--A};
\node[above right] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[left] at (O) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The radius $OM$ is perpendicular to chord $AB$ at $N$. Given $ON=5\text{ cm}$ and $NM=5\text{ cm}$, find the exact area between chord $AB$ and the minor arc $AB$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.28cm,y=0.28cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);\coordinate (A) at (60:10);\coordinate (B) at (-60:10);\coordinate (N) at (5,0);\coordinate (M) at (10,0);
\draw (O) circle (10);\draw (O)--(A) (O)--(B) (A)--(B) (O)--(M);
\draw (4.4,0)--(4.4,0.6)--(5,0.6);
\path (O) node[left] {$O$} (A) node[above right] {$A$} (B) node[below right] {$B$} (5,1.5) node[right] {$N$} (M) node[right] {$M$};
\path (2.5,0) node[below] {$5\text{ cm}$} (7.5,0) node[below] {$5\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A garden consists of triangle $ABD$ and sector $BCD$ with centre $B$, on opposite sides of $BD$. Given $AB=AD=4\text{ m}$, $\angle DAB=\dfrac{2\pi}{3}$ and $\angle DBC=\dfrac{\pi}{3}$, find the exact total area.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.55cm,y=0.55cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (B) at (0,0);\coordinate (D) at (0,6.928203);\coordinate (A) at (-2,3.464102);\coordinate (C) at (30:6.928203);
\draw (B)--(A)--(D)--cycle;\draw (B)--(C) arc[start angle=30,end angle=90,radius=6.928203];
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[below] {$B$} (C) node[right] {$C$} (D) node[above] {$D$};
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,left] {$4\text{ m}$};\path (A)--(D) node[midway,left] {$4\text{ m}$};
\draw (-1.6,2.771281) arc[start angle=-60,end angle=60,radius=0.8];\path (-0.9,3.464102) node {$\frac{2\pi}{3}$};
\draw (30:1.1) arc[start angle=30,end angle=90,radius=1.1];\path (60:1.7) node {$\frac{\pi}{3}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Triangle $ABC$ is equilateral with side length $3\text{ cm}$. Minor arcs $AB$, $BC$ and $CA$ have centres $C$, $A$ and $B$ respectively. Find the exact perimeter and area enclosed by the three arcs.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (B) at (0,0);\coordinate (C) at (3,0);\coordinate (A) at (1.5,2.598076);
\draw (A) arc[start angle=120,end angle=180,radius=3];\draw (B) arc[start angle=240,end angle=300,radius=3];\draw (C) arc[start angle=0,end angle=60,radius=3];
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\path (A) node[above] {$A$} (B) node[left] {$B$} (C) node[right] {$C$};\path (B)--(C) node[midway,above] {$3\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
